You ask a colleague to find the two items that need attention in
a report. They say, ``the red lines.'' On your screen the two
shades look alike. In a spoken reading of the terminal, there are
no red lines at all. The report does have a useful property: it is
text. If the status is written in words, you can read it with your
preferred font, search for it, or take it to a reader that suits you.

\begin{objectives}
\item Make keyboard, visual, and spoken access adjustments without
losing a known-good setup.
\item Turn a short report into plain, searchable output with explicit status.
\item Preserve the original report and verify the result independently of color.
\end{objectives}

\section{Distinguish the display from the data}\label{sec:44-layers}

When terminal output is hard to use, find where the obstacle lives.
The terminal application draws characters, manages a scrollback
buffer, and may offer an accessibility interface for a screen reader.
The shell handles the prompt and its own command editing. An
application you run may choose whether to print plain lines, move
the cursor, or take over the entire window. Chapter~\ref{ch:43}
isolated changes to a shell prompt; changing the font used to
display that prompt is normally a terminal-application setting.
Figure~\ref{fig:44-layers} separates these responsibilities.
\index{accessibility}\index{screen readers}

\input{figures/chapter-44/layers.tikz}

Make one change at a time, in a spare window or a duplicate terminal
profile if your application offers one. Record the old value and how
to switch back before changing it. Try the smallest test that uses
the setting you changed: read one prompt, move through a known
command by character, and read a line after that command finishes.
Check what happens when the window is not quite wide enough. A
visually wrapped line may still be one line in the file; verify this
by counting lines or by reading the file in a different tool.
Larger fonts may mean more wrap; that is a reason for shorter
labels, not a reason to give up the font you can read.

Think about a wrapped email address as a concrete example.
The extra screen row may be a display decision, or the original
text may have a real newline in the middle. Those are different
problems: width changes help with the first, but a valid address
still needs reconstruction in the second. A spoken reader might
announce both events as a pause. A test with known line breaks
makes that distinction easier to learn than trying to guess from
the pacing of an unfamiliar report. Long lines aren't inherently
unusable, but the person reading them should have a way to
reach the relevant portion and understand whether it belongs to
the previous line. When designing your own report, one short
record per line gives somebody a choice of reader without
making them reconstruct the layout.

\begin{quicktable}{p{.32\linewidth}Y}
\toprule
What makes work hard & First adjustment to test \\
\midrule
Characters look alike & A larger monospace font; test zero and
capital O, then one, lowercase l, and capital I. \\
Screen output vanishes & Use scrollback or save plain output and
read the saved file; check whether scrollback is limited. \\
Reader speaks every update & Avoid animated tools when a plain
report option is available; test the report in a separate reader. \\
Wrong app gets key & Check screen-reader and terminal
key bindings before changing shell editing bindings. \\
State is shown by shade alone & Add words such as ``open'' and
``done'' at the data or reporting layer. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

For visual settings, try high contrast between text and background
and evaluate normal, selected, and error text separately. A color
scheme is a set of several color pairs, not one. Try a roomy font at
the actual zoom you work at; check narrow punctuation as well as
letters. Turn off typographic ligatures if a font or renderer combines
two source characters into a confusing glyph. Test cursor visibility
when the window does not have focus; a flashing caret may not help
someone who is distracted by motion. A label such as \texttt{open}
keeps its meaning if you take the terminal's colors away.

For speech or braille, start with the documented interaction model
for your terminal and reader. Some readers expose a review mode
that lets you read an earlier line without moving the command's
cursor. Learn how to return to the live input position. On macOS,
Apple documents Terminal's accessibility settings and VoiceOver;
on Linux, the Orca guide describes reviewing text in accessible
applications. On the BSDs the availability of graphical terminals
and readers depends on what is installed. Do not assume the same
keystrokes or even the same access to live terminal output. A plain
text file offers another route on every one of these systems: use
an editor or reader you already know. None of the commands below
requires altering the global keyboard configuration.

Test typed input separately from program output. A reader that
echoes typed characters can help with accuracy but make long
input hard to review if it repeats everything simultaneously.
A synthesizer that pronounces a command name as a single word
may not speak a dash you need to copy. Read by character or
switch to a punctuation level that works for the test; you
needn't permanently announce every symbol in every app.
For braille, ask which part of a long line is visible after
panning and where the device indicates focus. When writing
instructions for others, provide a copyable literal command
and describe its components in words. ``Use the short dash
before the letter n'' is information a keyboard diagram may
not convey.

\section{Build a report you can read out of order}\label{sec:44-report}

The practice uses POSIX shell syntax; type \cmd|sh| first in a spare
terminal if needed. Create a fresh directory; if the assignment or
\cmd|cd| fails, stop rather than creating these files elsewhere.
The Xs in the template give \cmd|mktemp| a place to generate a
unique name. \cmd|>| will write or replace its destination. Here
the names are reserved for toy data, not an existing report.

\begin{terminal}
lab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/utp44.XXXXXX") &&
    cd "$lab" && lab=$PWD
pwd
printf '%s\n' 'open: call the library' 'done: return the book' \
    'open: choose next title' > ./tasks.txt
\end{terminal}

The same starting report is in
\FilePath{listings/chapter-44/tasks.txt}.

Use each status as part of the line, not as a separate legend.
Read only the beginning first. \cmd|head -n 2| asks for two
physical lines of text; multiple screen rows after a wrap are still
one physical line. Use \cmd|wc -l| with input redirection to count
newline characters in the entire report. The fixture has a final
newline so this is also the number of its lines. Redirection hides
the operand name from \cmd|wc|; some versions pad the number.

\begin{terminal}
head -n 2 ./tasks.txt
wc -l < ./tasks.txt
\end{terminal}

The first command should print the call and the return. The count is
three. An optional full-screen pager can be convenient, but start
with a short bounded read: a screen reader may speak a whole page
when that is not what you want. Pressing a key in a pager can move
its internal cursor, send shell input, or do neither, depending on
which program is in the foreground. Know how to leave a tool
before testing it. The optional \cmd|less| pager documents
\Key{q} for quit; this chapter does not require it.

Extract the open lines to a second file. The quoted pattern is a
basic regular expression: the caret requires a match at the
beginning of a line. This differs from searching for the letters
``open'' anywhere, which would find ``reopen'' if it were present.
\cmd|-n| prefixes each matched line with its original line number.
Line numbers let a collaborator locate evidence without reading the
report in the same screen order you did.

\begin{terminal}
grep -n '^open:' ./tasks.txt > ./open.txt
printf 'search status: %s\n' "$?"
cat ./open.txt
\end{terminal}

\begin{terminaloutput}
1:open: call the library
3:open: choose next title
\end{terminaloutput}

The expected search status is zero. Status one means no match;
greater than one indicates an error. Redirection can leave an empty
file even if a search fails, so do not interpret an empty result
without reading the diagnostic and status. \cmd|cat| prints the
saved output; it does not colorize the findings. Copy this small
fixture into your preferred plain text reader if the live window
has no suitable review mode. A graphical editor can be the right
last step even when the terminal is the right tool for filtering.
\index{head@\texttt{head}}\index{grep@\texttt{grep}}\index{wc@\texttt{wc}}

\input{figures/chapter-44/routes.tikz}

Figure~\ref{fig:44-routes} shows two ways to read the same result.
Saving output is not a universal switch to plain text. If a command
has already emitted terminal control characters, redirection can
store those same characters. Look in that command's own manual for
plain, no-color, or noninteractive options. There is no POSIX option
called \texttt{--no-color} that must work everywhere. Likewise
setting \texttt{NO\_COLOR} only affects software that honors that
convention. Removing color does not suppress animation, progress
output, or cursor motion on every utility.

You can review a saved report without moving the terminal
cursor through screen rows. Ask for its final matching line
and give it a spoken introductory sentence. \cmd|tail -n 1|
asks for the last line; it doesn't need a pager to do this.
The first command describes what you intend to read, and the
second prints the actual evidence. If the file is missing,
the label alone does not mean there was any evidence.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'Last open item in the saved report:'
tail -n 1 ./open.txt
\end{terminal}

Expect the line numbered three, not a line numbered two.
The number came from the source file and is intentionally
preserved. If the person reviewing the list requests only
one item at a time, labels and stable numbers let you do
that without asking them to rely on a particular cursor
position or reading speed. In contrast, a stream of partial
updates may show an unfinished result as though it were the
current answer and then overwrite it on the next redraw.

\section{Hear the result of a command, not only its lines}

Try making an affirmative status line for a no-match search.
\cmd|-q| tells \cmd|grep| to suppress matched lines; the exit status
is useful when only the presence of a match matters. With a single
controlled file, the cases below distinguish no result from an
error. For a search across many files where independent errors
matter, do not use quiet mode as proof that all files were readable:
a successful match can give zero even if another file failed.
Here the quoted caret pattern means to look for a line that starts
with the status \texttt{waiting:}.

\begin{terminal}
grep -q '^waiting:' ./tasks.txt
report_status=$?
case "$report_status" in
    0) printf '%s\n' 'Waiting items found.' ;;
    1) printf '%s\n' 'No waiting items.' ;;
    *) printf 'Search error, status %s.\n' "$report_status" ;;
esac
\end{terminal}

\cmd|case| chooses the first matching pattern for the value supplied,
and each \cmd|;;| ends a branch. The final \cmd|*| pattern catches
all other status values. \cmd|esac| ends the construct. Expect the
plain sentence ``No waiting items.'' This label and the match
lines each answer a different question: whether the request found
anything, and where an item appeared in the source. Both can work
without color. For a batch job, keep status lines short and make
the order predictable. A friendly sentence in an interactive
report can be a nuisance when mixed with machine-readable rows;
write status information to standard error in that setting
(Chapter~\ref{ch:15}).

A screen reader can read error messages too, but its timing
and the terminal's redraw can make one easy to miss. Preserve
a known diagnostic for review. The file \texttt{absent.txt}
was never created; the command is supposed to fail. \cmd|2>|
writes standard error into the named file, not into the report.
Record the status immediately and review the diagnostic as
text afterwards.

\begin{terminal}
head -n 1 ./absent.txt 2> ./diagnostic.txt
printf 'read status: %s\n' "$?"
cat ./diagnostic.txt
\end{terminal}

Expect a nonzero status and a diagnostic about the missing
path. Wording and punctuation vary. In a real workflow, make
sure your accessibility route preserves failures as well as
results; saving only standard output can lose the error that
explains a missing record. Keep the diagnostic separate from
data, and avoid sharing the file verbatim if it contains a
private path or an identifier. Saving it locally for review
doesn't mean consenting to publish it.

\begin{dialoguebox}{What counts as plain?}
\Brian{I turned off the colors and the report is still hard to use.}
\Stephen{What gets in the way now?}
\Brian{The reader starts again on every percentage update.}
\Stephen{Then I solved the wrong part. Plain colors don't imply stable lines.}
\Brian{I'd rather read the final count than hear it being made.}
\Stephen{Look for a noninteractive report and test it with your reader.}
\Brian{And put the status in words, so I can search for it tomorrow.}
\DialogueConclusion{Plain output needs stable, meaningful text.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Test a route from the keyboard}

Do not practice with private data, especially if a screen reader
speaks it aloud. Use this literal string to test how the font and
reader distinguish shapes. Hear the label and count the characters
if you need to tell whether the reader pronounced a spelling or
read a word. Different readers may speak the symbols differently.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'Zero and O: 0 O' 'One, ell, eye: 1 l I'
\end{terminal}

In the same test window, type but do not execute a simple
\cmd|printf '%s\n' 'practice:'| line. Use the documented keys for
your reader or shell to review the editable text. Compare its
contents before and after moving the caret, then execute it only
if the line still matches the harmless test. If the input has
changed in ways you cannot inspect, cancel rather than pressing
\EnterKey{}. In a normal interactive shell, \KeyChord{Ctrl}{C}
usually cancels the input, but verify which application currently
owns the keyboard. If a screen-reader shortcut doesn't deliver a
shell editing key, start by checking who owns the binding; do not
disable a critical access mechanism to make one shortcut work.
Figure~\ref{fig:44-keys} shows this diagnosis.

\input{figures/chapter-44/keys.tikz}

\begin{quicktable}{p{.29\linewidth}Y}
\toprule
Part of the trial & What to verify before keeping the change \\
\midrule
Input & Can you identify the live editing location and cancel an
uncertain line without submitting it? \\
Status & Can you tell a successful command, a no-match result, and
an error apart with the display configuration you use? \\
Navigation & Can you reread an earlier line and return to the
prompt? Are editor or reader shortcuts still available? \\
Recovery & Do you know the old setting and how to restore it without
relying on the newly changed shortcut? \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

Some people use a mouse to select a past line and a keyboard to
enter commands; others do the reverse or do neither. The useful
test is whether the exact tasks you need are possible, not whether
you can reproduce a particular person's setup. If you report an
accessibility gap, describe the smallest harmless input, what you
could perceive, and which component you expected to respond.
An audio report that says ``the reader says blank after the last
line'' can be more useful than a screenshot. Record application,
shell, and reader versions, not personal file contents.

\subsection{Keep an alternative when a single route is fragile}

Suppose you send a daily report to two people. One wants to
scroll through the list using the terminal's review mode; one
wants to search predictable text in an editor after the report
finishes. They can be reading the same facts. Do not ask the
second person to convert a screenshot into text or retrace
an animation to find what was actually decided. If a dashboard
is valuable, keep it, and also make the stable records
accessible. Decide what one record means: an item with a
status, a count for one category, or an error with an
identifiable source. Write the records so a later reader
doesn't need a legend visible in another pane.

Review an output file in a window unlike the one in which it
was made. Try different line lengths and a plain font. Can
you identify the open items from the text alone? Can you tell
whether a status line describes one item or the whole report?
If a table depends on columns lining up, put a few records
into a labeled one-per-line format and compare them aloud.
Neither layout is intrinsically better for every use. The
test is whether the information survives changing the tool
that reads it.

When a live program can't produce a usable result and you
cannot safely capture it, use a different tool to do the
same job. Accessibility is not a promise that every terminal
application works with every reader. Prefer a route you can
verify with your own setup over a clever configuration that
fails whenever a package changes. Write down the route and the
smallest example you can use to check it after an update.

\begin{takeaways}
\item Change the layer that owns the problem: display, shell, or application.
\item Save stable text and give important status a word, not only a color.
\item Test access and rollback with an ordinary, repeatable task.
\end{takeaways}

\begin{exercise}{An error is not an empty report}\label{ex:44-error}
Run the status example with a nonexistent file in place of
\texttt{tasks.txt}, using the name \texttt{absent.txt}. Read both the
diagnostic and the status line. Do not create that file.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Replace only the file operand on the \texttt{grep} line. The utility
should identify the missing file on standard error, with a status
greater than one. The final branch prints an error rather than
claiming there are no waiting items. No file was written.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Locate a line without color}\label{ex:44-locate}
Write only the numbered done line to \texttt{done.txt} and verify
that it is from the second line. Remove \texttt{done.txt} during
chapter cleanup.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
grep -n '^done:' ./tasks.txt > ./done.txt
cat ./done.txt
\end{terminal}
Expect \texttt{2:done: return the book}. The number is the source
line, not the line number inside the new file.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Change one display choice}\label{ex:44-display}
In a secondary profile or spare window, note the current font,
contrast, or reader setting. Change one setting, repeat the
character distinction test and the test of reaching the live
prompt, then return to the original setting. If you do not have an
independent profile or the change is unsafe, use a plain text
reader for \texttt{open.txt} and test a preference there instead.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
The controlled comparison is the same input before and after,
with a specific observation such as ``I can distinguish zero from
O'' or ``the reader now repeats the status.'' Revert even a helpful
setting as part of this exercise to demonstrate a recovery route;
then choose whether to reapply it. Close only the window you set
aside, not a working session. No additional chapter file is needed.
\end{solution}

Cleanup: check \cmd|pwd| in the practice shell first. Remove
the chapter's exact report names and leave the directory. Omit
\texttt{done.txt} unless you did that exercise. Leaving your
working shell is not required unless you opened a child for this
practice.

\begin{terminal}
rm ./tasks.txt ./open.txt ./diagnostic.txt ./done.txt
cd / && rmdir "$lab"
\end{terminal}

